  <updated>2026-09-26T20:52:15+00:00</updated>
  <media:group>
   <media:title>Colorado 150 – Kuner-Empson Cannery Building</media:title>
   <media:content url="https://www.youtube.com/v/Wzaf5myoetg?version=3" type="application/x-shockwave-flash" width="640" height="390"/>
   <media:thumbnail url="https://i4.ytimg.com/vi/Wzaf5myoetg/hqdefault.jpg" width="480" height="360"/>
   <media:description>We’re standing in front of the Kuner-Empson Cannery Building at 3rd Ave and Martin St. In 1883, Cincinnati businessman John H. Empson came to Longmont for his health, a common reason for relocating here. With a keen eye for business ideas, Empson established a commercial cannery for canning vegetables. This was a new industry due to recent advancements in metalworking.  

Empson’s Cannery permanently transformed the economy of Longmont. Farmers switched from growing wheat to vegetables, and seasonal canning jobs employed women and new immigrants. The Kuner-Empson Company's canned peas, beans, corn, and pumpkins would be distributed nationally for decades, until old equipment and an outdated sewer system forced its closure in 1970. 

In the 1980s, the cannery’s warehouse was readapted into the Cannery Apartments, which they still are today. 

Join us every Thursday for the rest of the year as we celebrate the triple anniversary of Longmont turning 155, Colorado turning 150, and America turning 250. We will share stories and photos on specific monthly themes tied to the history of the city we call home. This month, we’re shining the spotlight on Longmont’s history of food production.  

Visit LongmontColorado.gov/CO150 and check back every Thursday to learn more.  

#LongmontColorado #LongmontMuseum #LongmontHistory #VisitLongmont #Colorado150 #ThrowbackThursday #owndowntownlongmont</media:description>
